<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boris II of Bulgaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 931–977</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boris II (c. 931–977) was tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 969 to 971, succeeding his father Peter I during the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> in 971, Boris was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he was stripped of his regalia, and eastern Bulgaria was annexed. He escaped in 977 but was killed by a Bulgarian border guard who did not recognise him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boris-ii-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="Boris II of Bulgaria" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Skylitzes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boris_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
